completions_1:
The first basket has 10 eggs. The second basket has twice as many eggs as the first basket, so the second basket has 2 * 10 = 20 eggs.

To find the total number of eggs in both baskets, we add the number of eggs in each basket: 10 + 20 = 30.

\boxed{30}

completions_2:
First, we need to figure out how much line the caretaker needs in total. Since he needs 3 feet of line for every 1 foot of dock, and there are 200 feet of dock, he will need 3 * 200 = 600 feet of line.

Next, we need to figure out how much line he needs to buy. He already has 6 feet of line, and he needs 600 feet in total, so he needs to buy 600 - 6 = 594 feet of line.

\boxed{594}

completions_4:
The problem states that the caretaker needs 3 feet of line for every 1 foot of dock. The caretaker has 200 feet of dock, so he needs a total of (3 feet of line / 1 foot of dock) * 200 feet of dock = 600 feet of line.

He already has 6 feet of line, so he needs to buy the difference between the total line needed and the line he has: 600 feet - 6 feet = 594 feet.

\boxed{594}
